ch to kickstart a lush, healthy lawn. Learn about cleanup, mowing, fertilizing, weed control \\u0026amp; more.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"meta\\\",\\\"12\\\",{\\\"property\\\":\\\"og:image\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"https://s.alicdn.com/@sc02/kf/A585c7ea3131d43e78243fd07ea9e7555W.jpg\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"meta\\\",\\\"13\\\",{\\\"name\\\":\\\"twitter:card\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"summary_large_image\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"meta\\\",\\\"14\\\",{\\\"name\\\":\\\"twitter:title\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"Lawn Care Tips for March: Your Complete Early Spring Guide\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"meta\\\",\\\"15\\\",{\\\"name\\\":\\\"twitter:description\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"Discover essential lawn care tips for March to kickstart a lush, healthy lawn. Learn about cleanup, mowing, fertilizing, weed control \\u0026amp; more.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"meta\\\",\\\"16\\\",{\\\"name\\\":\\\"twitter:image\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/og-image.png\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"link\\\",\\\"17\\\",{\\\"rel\\\":\\\"icon\\\",\\\"href\\\":\\\"/icon.png?icon.00o_cnfbcjk.1.png\\\",\\\"sizes\\\":\\\"1024x1024\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"image/png\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L1e\\\",\\\"18\\\",{}]]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1f:I[5500,[\\\"/_next/static/chunks/0mkh1ezli734p.js\\\",\\\"/_next/static/chunks/0d3shmwh5_nmn.js\\\",\\\"/_next/static/chunks/05-hs12ys24cd.js\\\"],\\\"Image\\\"]\\n20:T55f,\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"[\\u0026_h1]:text-2xl [\\u0026_h1]:font-bold [\\u0026_h1]:mt-8 [\\u0026_h1]:mb-4 [\\u0026_h1]:text-[var(--color-foreground)] [\\u0026_h2]:text-2xl [\\u0026_h2]:font-bold [\\u0026_h2]:mt-8 [\\u0026_h2]:mb-4 [\\u0026_h2]:text-[var(--color-foreground)] [\\u0026_h3]:text-xl [\\u0026_h3]:font-semibold [\\u0026_h3]:mt-6 [\\u0026_h3]:mb-3 [\\u0026_h3]:text-[var(--color-foreground)] [\\u0026_h4]:text-lg [\\u0026_h4]:font-semibold [\\u0026_h4]:mt-4 [\\u0026_h4]:mb-2 [\\u0026_p]:mb-5 [\\u0026_p]:leading-7 [\\u0026_a]:text-[var(--color-primary)] [\\u0026_a]:underline [\\u0026_a:hover]:text-[var(--color-primary-dark)] [\\u0026_ul]:list-disc [\\u0026_ul]:ml-6 [\\u0026_ul]:mb-5 [\\u0026_ol]:list-decimal [\\u0026_ol]:ml-6 [\\u0026_ol]:mb-5 [\\u0026_li]:mb-2 [\\u0026_li]:leading-7 [\\u0026_blockquote]:border-l-4 [\\u0026_blockquote]:border-[var(--color-primary)] [\\u0026_blockquote]:pl-4 [\\u0026_blockquote]:my-6 [\\u0026_blockquote]:text-[var(--color-muted-fg)] [\\u0026_blockquote]:italic [\\u0026_table]:w-full [\\u0026_table]:border-collapse [\\u0026_table]:my-6 [\\u0026_table]:text-sm [\\u0026_thead]:bg-[var(--color-primary)] [\\u0026_th]:text-white [\\u0026_th]:px-4 [\\u0026_th]:py-3 [\\u0026_th]:text-left [\\u0026_th]:font-semibold [\\u0026_td]:px-4 [\\u0026_td]:py-3 [\\u0026_td]:border-b [\\u0026_td]:border-[var(--color-border)] [\\u0026_code]:bg-[var(--color-muted)] [\\u0026_code]:px-1.5 [\\u0026_code]:py-0.5 [\\u0026_code]:rounded [\\u0026_code]:text-sm [\\u0026_code]:font-mono [\\u0026_pre]:bg-[var(--color-muted)] [\\u0026_pre]:p-4 [\\u0026_pre]:rounded-lg [\\u0026_pre]:overflow-x-auto [\\u0026_pre]:my-6 [\\u0026_hr]:border-[var(--color-border)] [\\u0026_hr]:my-8 [\\u0026_strong]:font-semibold [\\u0026_strong]:text-[var(--color-foreground)] [\\u0026_em]:italic\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"21:T564f,\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"\\u003ch1\\u003eLawn Care Tips for March: Your Complete Early Spring Guide\\u003c/h1\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eMarch marks the beginning of the lawn care season for much of the country. As soil temperatures rise and daylight hours increase, your grass is waking from its winter dormancy and preparing for a season of vigorous growth. The work you put into your lawn during this transitional month sets the tone for the entire year ahead.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eWhether you maintain a \\u003ca href=\\\"/grass-types/bermuda-grass\\\"\\u003ewarm-season Bermuda grass\\u003c/a\\u003e lawn in the South or a \\u003ca href=\\\"/grass-types/kentucky-bluegrass\\\"\\u003ecool-season Kentucky bluegrass\\u003c/a\\u003e lawn in the North, March presents unique opportunities and challenges. Timing your tasks correctly can mean the difference between a thick, vibrant lawn and one that struggles with weeds, bare patches, and disease throughout the growing season.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eThis comprehensive guide walks you through every essential lawn care task for March, organized by priority and region, so you can build a strong foundation for a beautiful lawn all year long.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch2\\u003e1. Spring Lawn Cleanup: Clearing the Way for Growth\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eBefore you can focus on feeding, mowing, or treating your lawn, you need to remove the debris that accumulated over the winter months. A thorough spring cleanup is the single most important first step in your March lawn care routine.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eRake Away Dead Grass \\u0026amp; Debris\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eWinter leaves behind a layer of dead grass blades, fallen leaves, twigs, and other organic matter that can smother your lawn. This matted layer, sometimes called thatch buildup, blocks sunlight from reaching the soil surface and creates a damp environment that invites fungal diseases like snow mold.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eUse a sturdy \\u003cstrong\\u003eleaf rake\\u003c/strong\\u003e or a \\u003cstrong\\u003ethatching rake\\u003c/strong\\u003e to gently pull away dead material. For larger lawns, consider renting a \\u003cstrong\\u003epower rake\\u003c/strong\\u003e or \\u003cstrong\\u003edethatcher\\u003c/strong\\u003e to make the job faster. Be thorough but gentle — you want to remove dead material without tearing out living grass crowns that are just beginning to green up.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eRemove Winter Debris \\u0026amp; Inspect for Damage\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eWalk your entire lawn and pick up branches, rocks, and any trash that may have blown in during winter storms. Pay special attention to areas near roads and driveways where salt and sand may have accumulated. These areas often show signs of \\u003ca href=\\\"/lawn-care/salt-damage-repair\\\"\\u003esalt damage\\u003c/a\\u003e and may need extra attention.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eWhile cleaning, inspect your lawn for signs of:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cul\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eSnow mold\\u003c/strong\\u003e — circular gray or pink patches of matted grass\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eVole damage\\u003c/strong\\u003e — narrow tunnels or runways across the lawn surface\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eLow spots\\u003c/strong\\u003e — areas where water pooled over the winter\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eBare patches\\u003c/strong\\u003e — spots where grass died back completely\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eIdentifying these problems early allows you to address them before the growing season is fully underway. For detailed guidance on diagnosing common issues, check out our \\u003ca href=\\\"/lawn-care/lawn-disease-identification\\\"\\u003elawn disease identification guide\\u003c/a\\u003e.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eTools You Will Need for Spring Cleanup\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cul\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eLeaf rake\\u003c/strong\\u003e (flexible tines for gentle raking)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eThatching rake\\u003c/strong\\u003e (for moderate thatch removal)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eLawn sweeper\\u003c/strong\\u003e or \\u003cstrong\\u003epower rake\\u003c/strong\\u003e (for large properties)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eGarden gloves\\u003c/strong\\u003e and \\u003cstrong\\u003eknee pads\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eWheelbarrow\\u003c/strong\\u003e or \\u003cstrong\\u003eyard waste bags\\u003c/strong\\u003e for debris removal\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch2\\u003e2. Mowing in March: Setting the Right Height \\u0026amp; Schedule\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eOne of the most common questions homeowners ask is when to start mowing in the spring. The answer depends on your grass type and local climate, but there are universal principles that apply everywhere.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eWhen to Make Your First Cut\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eWait until your grass has actively started growing and has reached at least \\u003cstrong\\u003eone-third higher than your target mowing height\\u003c/strong\\u003e. For most cool-season grasses, this means waiting until blades are about 3.5 to 4 inches tall before cutting them back to 2.5 to 3 inches. For warm-season grasses emerging from dormancy, the first mowing typically happens when the grass reaches about 2 to 2.5 inches.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eRushing to mow too early can stress grass that is still establishing its root system for the season. Patience during this transition period pays off with stronger, more resilient turf.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eSharpen Your Mower Blades\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eBefore your first mow of the season, \\u003cstrong\\u003esharpen or replace your mower blades\\u003c/strong\\u003e. Dull blades tear grass rather than cutting it cleanly, leaving ragged edges that turn brown and make your lawn more susceptible to disease. A sharp blade produces a clean cut that heals quickly and maintains a uniform, healthy appearance.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003ePlan to sharpen your mower blades at least twice per season — once at the beginning of spring and again around midsummer. If you mow a large lawn or frequently encounter twigs and debris, you may need to sharpen them more often.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eMowing Heights by Grass Type\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eSetting the correct mowing height is critical for lawn health. Here is a quick reference for common grass types:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cul\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/grass-types/kentucky-bluegrass\\\"\\u003eKentucky Bluegrass\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/strong\\u003e: 2.5 to 3.5 inches\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/grass-types/fine-fescue\\\"\\u003eFine Fescue\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/strong\\u003e: 2.5 to 4 inches\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/grass-types/tall-fescue\\\"\\u003eTall Fescue\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/strong\\u003e: 3 to 4 inches\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/grass-types/perennial-ryegrass\\\"\\u003ePerennial Ryegrass\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/strong\\u003e: 2 to 3 inches\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/grass-types/bermuda-grass\\\"\\u003eBermuda Grass\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/strong\\u003e: 1 to 2 inches\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/grass-types/zoysia-grass\\\"\\u003eZoysia Grass\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/strong\\u003e: 1 to 2.5 inches\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/grass-types/st-augustine-grass\\\"\\u003eSt. Augustine Grass\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/strong\\u003e: 2.5 to 4 inches\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/grass-types/centipede-grass\\\"\\u003eCentipede Grass\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/strong\\u003e: 1.5 to 2.5 inches\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eNever remove more than one-third of the grass blade in a single mowing. If your lawn has grown taller than expected during a warm spell, gradually reduce the height over two or three mowing sessions rather than scalping it all at once.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eLeave the Clippings\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eUnless your lawn is plagued with disease or the clippings are excessively long and clumping, leave them on the lawn. Grass clippings decompose quickly and return valuable nitrogen and organic matter to the soil. This simple practice, often called \\u003cstrong\\u003egrasscycling\\u003c/strong\\u003e, can supply up to 25 percent of your lawn's annual nitrogen needs.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch2\\u003e3. Fertilizing \\u0026amp; Soil Care: Feeding Your Lawn for the Season\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eMarch is an excellent time to evaluate your soil health and begin a fertilization program tailored to your grass type and regional conditions. Proper nutrition in early spring fuels strong root development and helps your lawn green up faster.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eTest Your Soil Before Fertilizing\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eBefore applying any fertilizer, conduct a \\u003cstrong\\u003esoil test\\u003c/strong\\u003e to determine your lawn's pH level and nutrient profile. Most grasses thrive in soil with a pH between 6.0 and 7.0. If your soil is too acidic (below 6.0), you may need to apply lime. If it is too alkaline (above 7.5), sulfur amendments can help bring it into range.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eSoil test kits are available at most garden centers, or you can send samples to your local cooperative extension service for a detailed analysis. Testing every two to three years is sufficient for most lawns. Learn more about understanding your results in our \\u003ca href=\\\"/lawn-care/soil-testing-guide\\\"\\u003ecomplete soil testing guide\\u003c/a\\u003e.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eChoosing the Right Spring Fertilizer\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eThe type of fertilizer you apply in March depends on your grass type:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cul\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eCool-season grasses\\u003c/strong\\u003e (Kentucky bluegrass, fescues, ryegrass): Apply a \\u003cstrong\\u003elight application\\u003c/strong\\u003e of balanced or nitrogen-rich fertilizer in early to mid-spring. A slow-release granular fertilizer with an N-P-K ratio such as 20-5-10 or 24-4-12 works well. Avoid heavy nitrogen applications, which can promote excessive top growth at the expense of root development and increase disease susceptibility.\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eWarm-season grasses\\u003c/strong\\u003e (Bermuda, Zoysia, St. Augustine, Centipede): Hold off on fertilizing until the grass has fully greened up and is actively growing, typically in late March to early April in warmer zones. Applying fertilizer too early can feed winter weeds and encourage tender growth that may be damaged by a late frost.\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eApplying Lime or Sulfur\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eIf your soil test indicates a pH imbalance, March is an ideal time to apply corrective amendments. Pelletized lime raises soil pH and is easy to spread with a standard broadcast spreader. Granular sulfur lowers pH for alkaline soils. Both amendments take several weeks to months to fully take effect, so applying them early in the season gives them time to work before peak growing conditions arrive.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eProduct Recommendations for Early Spring\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cul\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eSlow-release granular fertilizer\\u003c/strong\\u003e (e.g., 20-5-10 for cool-season lawns)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003ePelletized lime\\u003c/strong\\u003e (for acidic soils below pH 6.0)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eSoil sulfur\\u003c/strong\\u003e (for alkaline soils above pH 7.5)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eOrganic options\\u003c/strong\\u003e: Milorganite, Espoma Organic Lawn Food, or compost top-dressing\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eDrop or broadcast spreader\\u003c/strong\\u003e for even application\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eAlways follow the application rates listed on the product label. Over-fertilizing is one of the most common lawn care mistakes and can lead to thatch buildup, fertilizer burn, and increased pest activity.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch2\\u003e4. Weed Control \\u0026amp; Pest Prevention in March\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eMarch is a critical window for weed control. Many common lawn weeds germinate in early spring, and addressing them before they become established saves significant time and effort later in the season.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eApply Pre-Emergent Herbicides\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eOne of the most impactful things you can do in March is apply a \\u003cstrong\\u003epre-emergent herbicide\\u003c/strong\\u003e to prevent crabgrass and other annual weeds from germinating. The key to pre-emergent success is timing — you must apply the product \\u003cstrong\\u003ebefore soil temperatures consistently reach 55°F (13°C)\\u003c/strong\\u003e at a depth of about two inches.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eA good natural indicator is the blooming of forsythia bushes. When forsythia blooms in your area, crabgrass germination is likely just a week or two away, making it the perfect time to apply your pre-emergent.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eCommon pre-emergent active ingredients include:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cul\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eProdiamine\\u003c/strong\\u003e — long-lasting residual control (up to 6 months)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eDithiopyr\\u003c/strong\\u003e — offers both pre-emergent and early post-emergent control\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003ePendimethalin\\u003c/strong\\u003e — effective against a broad spectrum of annual grasses and broadleaf weeds\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eCorn gluten meal\\u003c/strong\\u003e — an organic alternative, though less potent than synthetic options\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eImportant note: Do not apply pre-emergent herbicides if you plan to \\u003ca href=\\\"/lawn-care/overseeding-guide\\\"\\u003eoverseed your lawn\\u003c/a\\u003e in the spring. These products prevent all grass seed from germinating, not just weed seeds. If overseeding is a priority, skip the pre-emergent and focus on post-emergent weed control instead.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eTackle Existing Weeds\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eFor weeds that are already visible — such as dandelions, chickweed, henbit, and wild violet — spot-treat them with a \\u003cstrong\\u003eselective post-emergent herbicide\\u003c/strong\\u003e labeled for use on your grass type. Applying herbicide on a calm, dry day when temperatures are between 50°F and 80°F (10°C to 27°C) ensures the best results.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eFor small infestations, hand-pulling is an effective and chemical-free option. Use a \\u003cstrong\\u003estand-up weeder tool\\u003c/strong\\u003e or a \\u003cstrong\\u003edandelion digger\\u003c/strong\\u003e to remove the entire root system and prevent regrowth.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eWatch for Early-Season Pests\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eMarch is also a time to watch for signs of insect activity. Grubs that overwintered in the soil may begin feeding on grass roots as temperatures warm. If you notice irregular brown patches that lift easily from the soil (indicating root damage), you may have a grub problem.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eFor grub control, consider applying a \\u003cstrong\\u003epreventative grub control product\\u003c/strong\\u003e containing chlorantraniliprole in early spring. This active ingredient is effective against grubs while being safer for beneficial insects and pollinators compared to older chemical options. For more information, visit our \\u003ca href=\\\"/lawn-care/grub-control-guide\\\"\\u003egrub control guide\\u003c/a\\u003e.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch2\\u003e5. Overseeding, Aeration \\u0026amp; Repairing Winter Damage\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eIf your lawn has thin areas, bare patches, or suffered significant winter damage, March can be a good time to repair and rejuvenate your turf — particularly for cool-season grass lawns.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eOverseeding Cool-Season Lawns\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eWhile fall is the ideal time to overseed cool-season grasses, spring overseeding can be effective if done early enough. The goal is to establish new grass before the heat and drought stress of summer arrives.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eFollow these steps for successful spring overseeding:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003col\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eMow low\\u003c/strong\\u003e: Cut existing grass to about 1.5 to 2 inches to allow sunlight to reach the soil and new seedlings.\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eRake thoroughly\\u003c/strong\\u003e: Remove thatch and debris to expose bare soil for seed contact.\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eCore aerate\\u003c/strong\\u003e (if possible): Aeration creates small holes in the soil that improve seed-to-soil contact and relieve compaction. Learn more in our \\u003ca href=\\\"/lawn-care/lawn-aeration-guide\\\"\\u003elawn aeration guide\\u003c/a\\u003e.\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eSpread seed\\u003c/strong\\u003e: Use a broadcast spreader to apply grass seed at the recommended rate for your chosen variety. Select a seed blend that matches your existing lawn — see our \\u003ca href=\\\"/grass-types\\\"\\u003egrass types guide\\u003c/a\\u003e for help choosing the right seed.\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eTop-dress lightly\\u003c/strong\\u003e: Apply a thin layer (about 1/4 inch) of compost or topsoil over the seeded areas to protect the seed and retain moisture.\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eWater consistently\\u003c/strong\\u003e: Keep seeded areas moist by watering lightly two to three times per day until seedlings reach about 2 inches tall.\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003c/ol\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eRepairing Bare Patches\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eFor small bare spots (less than a few feet across), you can use a \\u003cstrong\\u003epatch repair product\\u003c/strong\\u003e that combines grass seed, mulch, and fertilizer in one convenient mix. Simply loosen the soil in the bare area with a garden fork, spread the patch mix according to label directions, and keep the area consistently moist until new grass is established.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eShould You Aerate in March?\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eCore aeration is beneficial for lawns with compacted soil or heavy thatch layers. For cool-season grasses, early spring aeration (March to early April) can be effective, though fall remains the preferred time. For warm-season grasses, wait until late spring or early summer when the grass is actively growing and can recover quickly from the aeration process.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eSigns your lawn may need aeration include:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cul\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003eWater pooling on the surface after rainfall\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003eDifficulty pushing a screwdriver or probe into the soil\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003eHeavy foot traffic areas that appear thin and compacted\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003eThatch layers thicker than half an inch\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eLeveling Uneven Areas\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eWinter frost heave and settling can create bumps and low spots in your lawn. March is a good time to address these issues by top-dressing low areas with a mixture of sand and topsoil. Apply no more than half an inch at a time, allowing the grass to grow through the new layer before adding more. For significant grading issues, you may need to remove sod, regrade the soil, and replace the sod — a project best tackled when the grass is actively growing.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch2\\u003eFrequently Asked Questions About March Lawn Care\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eIs it too early to fertilize my lawn in March?\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eFor cool-season grasses such as Kentucky bluegrass, tall fescue, and perennial ryegrass, a light application of fertilizer in mid-to-late March is appropriate and beneficial. Use a slow-release formula and follow recommended application rates to avoid excessive top growth. For warm-season grasses like Bermuda, Zoysia, and St. Augustine, it is generally too early to fertilize in March unless you live in the warmest zones (USDA zones 9 and above) and your grass has fully broken dormancy. Fertilizing warm-season grass too early can promote weed growth and make tender new shoots vulnerable to late frosts.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eWhen should I apply pre-emergent herbicide in March?\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eApply pre-emergent herbicide when soil temperatures at a two-inch depth are consistently approaching 55°F (13°C). This typically falls somewhere in early to mid-March for the transition zone and southern regions, and late March to early April for northern areas. A helpful phenological indicator is the blooming of forsythia bushes — when you see those bright yellow flowers, it is time to apply your pre-emergent within the next week or two. Remember that pre-emergent herbicides will also prevent grass seed from germinating, so do not use them if you plan to overseed your lawn in the spring.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eShould I rake my lawn in March or wait until it dries out?\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eIt is best to wait until your lawn has dried out sufficiently before raking. Raking a soggy, saturated lawn can pull up healthy grass, damage crowns, and compact the soil further. A simple test is to walk on your lawn — if your footprints sink deeply and the soil feels spongy, wait another week or two. If the ground feels firm and the surface is mostly dry, gentle raking to remove dead material and debris is safe and beneficial. In northern climates where snow cover persists into March, wait until all snow has melted and the lawn has had several days to drain before beginning any raking or cleanup.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eCan I overseed my lawn in March?\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eYes, you can overseed cool-season lawns in March, and early spring overseeding can fill in thin areas and repair winter damage effectively. The key is to start early enough that new grass establishes before summer heat arrives. Prepare the soil by mowing low, raking, and ideally core aerating before spreading seed. Keep seeded areas consistently moist until seedlings are established. However, if you plan to use pre-emergent herbicide for crabgrass prevention, you will need to choose between overseeding and weed prevention, as the two practices are incompatible. For warm-season lawns, wait until late spring or early summer to overseed, when soil temperatures are consistently above 65°F (18°C).\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eHow often should I water my lawn in March?\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eIn most regions, natural rainfall in March is sufficient to meet your lawn's water needs. However, if you experience an unusually dry March with little precipitation, supplemental watering may be necessary. Apply about half an inch to one inch of water per week, either through rainfall or irrigation, to support early spring green-up. If you have newly overseeded areas, those spots will need more frequent, lighter watering — typically two to three brief watering sessions per day — to keep the seed and soil surface consistently moist. Avoid heavy watering that creates puddles or runoff, as this can wash away seed and promote fungal diseases. Always water in the early morning to minimize evaporation and reduce the risk of overnight moisture that encourages disease.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch2\\u003eRegional Timing Tips for March Lawn Care\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eBecause climate varies dramatically across the country, here is a quick regional breakdown to help you time your March lawn care tasks appropriately:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eNorthern Regions (USDA Zones 3–5)\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eSnow may still be on the ground in early March. Focus on late-March tasks such as initial cleanup, light raking once the lawn dries, and preparing your mower for the season. Soil temperatures are typically still too cold for fertilization or pre-emergent application. Be patient and let the lawn wake up naturally.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eTransition Zone (USDA Zones 5–7)\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eThis is where timing is most critical. Mid-to-late March is usually the sweet spot for cleanup, first mowing, light fertilization of cool-season lawns, and pre-emergent herbicide application. Monitor soil temperatures and watch for forsythia blooms as your natural cue.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch3\\u003eSouthern Regions (USDA Zones 7–10)\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eWarm-season lawns may already be breaking dormancy by early March. Begin cleanup and mowing as soon as growth starts. Apply pre-emergent herbicide early in the month. Hold off on fertilizing warm-season grasses until they have fully greened up, usually late March to early April. Cool-season lawns in the South (such as overseeded ryegrass) should be mowed regularly and can receive a light fertilizer application.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003ch2\\u003eFinal Thoughts\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eMarch lawn care is all about preparation and setting the stage for a healthy growing season. By cleaning up winter debris, sharpening your mower, testing your soil, applying the right fertilizer, preventing weeds, and repairing damage, you give your lawn the best possible start to the year.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eRemember that every lawn is unique. Your grass type, soil conditions, local climate, and the specific challenges your lawn faces will determine which tasks take priority. When in doubt, consult our \\u003ca href=\\\"/lawn-care\\\"\\u003ecomplete lawn care guide\\u003c/a\\u003e for season-by-season advice, or explore our \\u003ca href=\\\"/grass-types\\\"\\u003egrass types library\\u003c/a\\u003e to learn more about the specific needs of your turf.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n\\u003cp\\u003eStart with the basics this March, stay consistent with your maintenance routine, and you will be rewarded with a thick, green, weed-free lawn that you can enjoy all season long.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"15:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"max-w-[1280px] mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-10\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"script\\\",null,{\\\"type\\\":\\\"application/ld+json\\\",\\\"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\\\":{\\\"__html\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"@context\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"https://schema.org\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"@type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Article\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"headline\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Lawn Care Tips for March: Your Complete Early Spring Guide\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"description\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Discover essential lawn care tips for March to kickstart a lush, healthy lawn. Learn about cleanup, mowing, fertilizing, weed control \\u0026amp; more.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"image\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"https://s.alicdn.com/@sc02/kf/A585c7ea3131d43e78243fd07ea9e7555W.jpg\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"datePublished\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2026-07-23 00:20:53\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"dateModified\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2026-07-23 00:20:53\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"author\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"@type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Person\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"James Miller\\\\\\\"}}\\\"}}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"max-w-[800px] mx-auto\\\",\\\"ch